# User-facing help text for the `splitters` command line.
#
# This catalogue is the only owner of the prose a reader sees. Rust doc
# comments describe semantics for developers and are never rendered, because
# every declared command, argument, and about text resolves here. Identifiers
# are generated by the OrthoConfig derive; `src/cli/tests/catalogue.rs` proves
# this file declares exactly the generated set and nothing else.

splitters-cli-about = Split one Git branch into smaller, independently reviewable branches.
splitters-cli-long_about = Split one Git branch into smaller, independently reviewable branches.

splitters-cli-map-about = Decompose the current branch into fragments and write a manifest.
splitters-cli-map-args-base-help = Base ref to compare against
splitters-cli-map-args-manifest_dir-help = Directory that receives the manifest, fragments, and metadata

splitters-cli-validate-about = Prove that one proposal can be extracted cleanly.
splitters-cli-validate-args-proposal-help = Identifier of the proposal to validate
splitters-cli-validate-args-manifest_dir-help = Directory holding the manifest to read
splitters-cli-validate-args-base-help = Base ref to compare against
splitters-cli-validate-args-check-help = Command to run in both the candidate and residual worktrees

splitters-cli-extract-about = Move one validated proposal onto a new branch.
splitters-cli-extract-args-proposal-help = Identifier of the proposal to extract
splitters-cli-extract-args-manifest_dir-help = Directory holding the manifest to read
splitters-cli-extract-args-base-help = Base ref to compare against
splitters-cli-extract-args-branch-help = Name of the branch that receives the proposal
splitters-cli-extract-args-publish-help = Publish the extracted branch as a pull request after local extraction